+# Negotiated Merklix reconciliation and frozen epochs
+
+This experimental relay protocol does not alter transaction or block consensus
+identifiers. The shared engine retains ABC's SHA256/SHA256d identifiers and
+80-byte headers; Lotus V2 adaptations retain their native identifiers and roots.
+Ordinary transaction and block relay remains available when a reconciliation
+scope cannot be represented within the bounds below.
+
+## Capabilities and framing
+
+Peers opt in with NODE_MERKLIX (service bit 26). The original summary and bucket
+payload layouts remain unchanged. The bucket request command is `getmrklbckt`
+(11 bytes). The inherited spelling `getmerklixbuck` was 14 bytes and could not
+fit ABC's 12-byte command field; it was never a valid framed wire command.
+Direct handler recognition of that old spelling is retained only for callers
+that bypass framing. It is excluded from the valid message enumeration.
+
+NODE_MERKLIX_BATCH (bit 27) negotiates new `getmrklbatch` and `mrklbatch`
+commands. NODE_MERKLIX_SIZED (bit 29, requiring bit 27) negotiates
+`merklixsket`. Their vector encodings are distinct from the legacy payloads;
+old peers are not silently reinterpreted or excluded from ordinary relay.
+Local switches `-merklixreconciliation`, `-merklixbatch`, and `-merklixsized`
+control advertisement and use of the corresponding extensions.
+
+## Live sets, snapshots, and rounds
+
+The production send loop initiates live mempool reconciliation, with a minimum
+100 ms cadence and a one-second unchanged-root retry. Peers agree on a 256-bit
+transaction-set root. Sets use 16 top-level buckets, then **16-way nibble
+sub-buckets**, at depths 8, 12, and 16. A cell carries count and GF(2^64)
+syndromes S1/S3/S5; its decoder recovers at most two differing IDs. S5 checks a
+candidate solution; it does not increase recovery capacity.
+
+Batch-capable peers exchange one bounded reply vector and at most one short-ID
+request and one vector of descents for that reply. An arbitrary-salt or terminal
+bucket can still require a bounded scan. Default-salt depth-8/depth-12 syndromes
+are maintained incrementally on transaction additions and removals; their
+16-cell summary is a copy. Frozen/scoped sets retain separate indexed snapshots.
+
+Sized initiation estimates the difference as
+`abs(local_size - remote_size) + q * min(local_size, remote_size)`, where q comes
+from previous complete-list comparisons. It pushes depth-8 sketches at expected
+difference <=128, depth-12 at <=2048, and otherwise a complete short-ID list.
+There is no preliminary top-level-summary round trip in this mode. The root
+still detects equality. This list option intentionally spends bytes to avoid
+descents; measurements below show a substantial bandwidth cost.
+
+The native 10,000-transaction exact-scope test exercises the complete-list
+option, real GETSHORTTX/SHORTTX transport parsing, transaction admission, and
+final 256-bit root equality. For differences 10/100/1000/2000/5000, three
+repetitions measured median request/response rounds 1/1/1/1/2. This establishes
+the list fallback's requested round gate. It does not establish those medians
+for every adaptive sketch selection, adversarial short-ID collision, live-set
+churn, or a WAN. The final root prevents short-ID collisions from establishing
+false convergence; failed/incomplete attempts can retry or use ordinary relay.
+
+## Bounds and backpressure
+
+A complete scope is limited to 65,536 transactions and 32 MiB serialized bytes;
+the scoped cache is limited to 64 scopes and 64 MiB. Root normalization itself
+never silently truncates a set. Overflow declines that reconciliation scope,
+with ordinary relay/block fallback, rather than advertising a partial root as
+complete. The candidate cache separately bounds entries, parents, and bytes.
+
+Batch request vectors contain at most 256 requests; replies contain at most
+4096 summaries, with 2 MiB framing bounds. Short-ID requests contain at most
+4096 IDs. Larger complete-list differences progress through chunks, with at
+most 65,536 pending IDs per peer and 1,048,576 globally, and a 10-second timeout.
+Only complete-list transfers whose final full root matches enter the RPC round
+histogram. That histogram is not a count of all reconciliation rounds.
+
+Admission budgets charge bytes and transaction/cell visits, with separate
+sketch and transaction-payload buckets. Over-budget messages enter bounded
+FIFO queues (256 messages/2 MiB per peer, 16 MiB globally). Queue overflow
+explicitly disconnects the peer. Unsolicited bucket replies remain rejected.
+Payload responses queue transaction references (32 MiB per peer, 64 MiB global,
+16 jobs per peer), respect socket send backpressure, and emit bounded frames.
+Peer teardown releases its queues, authorizations, histories and pending IDs.
+
+## Epoch cadence and production integration
+
+The settling-window floor is `max(population p90 peer RTT, -epochminms)`, with
+`-epochminms=100` by default and a one-second ceiling. Each peer contributes its
+latest RTT sample, rather than repeatedly overweighting a fast peer. A nonempty
+tail waits for the window unless the 10,000-transaction memory cap is reached.
+Misses/conflicts back off the window; clean finalizations recover it.
+`getavalancheinfo.epochs` exposes the window, p90 floor, freeze count and rate.
+
+`MERKLIX_FREEZE_EPOCH_US` of approximately **21.9 ms on 10,000 transactions**
+is a historical benchmark of the cost of `FreezeEpochBatch()`. It is not the
+epoch interval and does not establish throughput or WAN finality.
+
+NODE_MERKLIX_EPOCH (bit 28) explicitly negotiates unchanged AVABATCH payloads
+plus exact epoch-scope reconciliation. Production startup requires an
+established Avalanche quorum, preconsensus activation including a finalized
+chain tip, and completion of initial block download. The send loop consults the
+controller, freezes an immutable snapshot, announces AVABATCH, and pushes its
+named EPOCH_BATCH sketch. A slot has at most one local in-flight proposal.
+The receiver retrieves an exact scope through the same reconciliation handlers;
+acceptance requires admitted transactions, parent/dependency closure, both
+transaction and UTXO roots, and the ordered-ID digest. Matching transaction
+count/root alone is insufficient.
+
+Referenced frozen snapshots are retained until the parent changes. Additional
+freezes stop at 64 snapshots, 65,536 aggregate retained transaction entries,
+or 64 MiB referenced serialized transactions. This bounds retained work/memory
+without deleting snapshots still named by mining/finalization. It can suppress
+new epochs before the next block under sustained load; ordinary relay remains
+available. This is a conservative retention bound, not demonstrated sustained
+epoch throughput.
+
+## Reproducible evidence and limits
+
+`p2p_merklix_initiation.py` seeds divergent mempools while nodes are disconnected,
+connects three nodes, and checks production reconciliation commands and
+convergence. Its disabled-capability control stays divergent over the same
+short interval. Legacy, batch-only and mixed-capability modes check negotiation.
+`p2p_merklix_limits.py` checks bounded deferral, draining and flood disconnection.
+Native tests cover framing, normalization bounds, incremental recomputation,
+batched message counts, complete-list rounds, bounded payloads and timeout
+cleanup. The native epoch test separately exercises the production send-loop
+trigger and exact-scope root rejection; it is not real quorum finalization.
+
+`merklix_netem_bench.py` runs three native regtest processes through disposable
+Linux Docker TCP forwarders. Only container eth0 qdiscs receive delay/loss;
+host networking and production nodes are untouched. The 20/80/200 ms configured
+RTT crossed with 0/1% loss was run with 30 transactions per case. This is real
+injected packet impairment, **not a real WAN**. RPC sampling adds observation
+overhead and ordinary INV relay runs concurrently, so timing alone is not
+causal proof of reconciliation.
+
+Final R2 matrix p50 was 185–817 ms, with about 27–34 KiB sent per transaction.
+The retained INV-only baseline used about 0.5 KiB per transaction. Reconciliation
+therefore has a substantial bandwidth regression in this small-transaction
+workload; no overall performance improvement or 100k TPS claim follows.
+Measured admission deferrals/rejections were zero in that matrix. The harness
+reports general per-reconciliation rounds and silent drops as unmeasured, and
+records the root-confirmed complete-list histogram separately. Avalanche is
+disabled in this relay benchmark, so epoch freeze rate and quorum/WAN epoch
+latency remain unmeasured. Before/after matrices and exact binary/source
+provenance belong with the delivery evidence, not an invented benchmark result.
